1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,360
Welcome to Rashi in Brief, the podcast where

2
00:00:03,408 --> 00:00:06,162
each week we explore the Torah portion through

3
00:00:06,202 --> 00:00:09,442
the eyes of Rashi, the great medieval commentator

4
00:00:09,509 --> 00:00:12,882
whose brilliant, concise readings have shaped how Jews

5
00:00:12,914 --> 00:00:15,442
have understood the Bible for nearly a thousand

6
00:00:15,535 --> 00:00:16,161
years.

7
00:00:16,354 --> 00:00:20,402
This week we're looking at Parashat Bereshit, the

8
00:00:20,466 --> 00:00:23,573
very first portion in the Torah, Genesis chapter

9
00:00:23,773 --> 00:00:26,893
one through chapter six verse eight, and there

10
00:00:26,946 --> 00:00:29,053
is no better place to begin than at

11
00:00:29,093 --> 00:00:31,773
the beginning itself — with Rashi as our

12
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:38,265
guide.Parashat Bereshit covers an enormous sweep of narrative.

13
00:00:38,558 --> 00:00:40,985
It opens with the creation of the world

14
00:00:41,038 --> 00:00:43,305
in six days and God's rest on the

15
00:00:43,375 --> 00:00:44,425
seventh.

16
00:00:44,612 --> 00:00:46,825
It then zooms in on the creation of

17
00:00:46,889 --> 00:00:49,225
Adam and Eve, their life in the Garden

18
00:00:49,252 --> 00:00:52,105
of Eden, the temptation of the serpent, and

19
00:00:52,132 --> 00:00:53,945
their expulsion from paradise.

20
00:00:54,153 --> 00:00:57,465
From there, we read about Cain and Abel

21
00:00:57,665 --> 00:01:01,385
— the first siblings and the first murder

22
00:01:01,545 --> 00:01:03,164
— and then the Torah races through ten

23
00:01:03,224 --> 00:01:06,204
generations of humanity, ending with the birth of

24
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:09,084
Noah and a world so corrupt that God

25
00:01:09,154 --> 00:01:10,764
regrets having created it.

26
00:01:10,991 --> 00:01:15,724
These are foundational stories, dense with meaning, and

27
00:01:15,777 --> 00:01:17,964
Rashi finds questions everywhere.

28
00:01:18,284 --> 00:01:21,324
He notices unusual word choices in the very

29
00:01:21,391 --> 00:01:22,284
first verse.

30
00:01:22,551 --> 00:01:25,244
He asks why the Torah begins where it

31
00:01:25,324 --> 00:01:25,724
does.

32
00:01:26,017 --> 00:01:29,244
He probes the psychology of the characters —

33
00:01:29,276 --> 00:01:33,164
what motivated Eve, what was Cain really feeling,

34
00:01:33,224 --> 00:01:35,564
how did God speak to them and why

35
00:01:35,644 --> 00:01:37,924
in that particular way.

36
00:01:38,151 --> 00:01:41,644
In a portion this rich, Rashi's comments range

37
00:01:41,676 --> 00:01:44,524
from the grammatical to the theological, and today

38
00:01:44,577 --> 00:01:47,004
we'll walk through several of his most striking

39
00:01:47,072 --> 00:01:51,793
observations.Let's start right at the very first verse

40
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:55,876
of the Torah: "In the beginning, God created

41
00:01:55,916 --> 00:01:59,316
the heavens and the earth." You might think

42
00:01:59,364 --> 00:02:02,756
this is a simple, declarative statement, but Rashi

43
00:02:02,809 --> 00:02:06,836
immediately raises a profound question — why does

44
00:02:06,876 --> 00:02:09,078
the Torah begin with the story of creation

45
00:02:09,131 --> 00:02:09,718
at all?

46
00:02:09,998 --> 00:02:13,559
The Torah, after all, is primarily a book

47
00:02:13,612 --> 00:02:16,359
of laws, of commandments given to the Jewish

48
00:02:16,416 --> 00:02:16,919
people.

49
00:02:17,079 --> 00:02:19,879
Wouldn't it have made more sense to begin

50
00:02:19,911 --> 00:02:22,359
with the first commandment given to Israel as

51
00:02:22,399 --> 00:02:22,999
a nation?

52
00:02:23,452 --> 00:02:27,964
Rashi, citing Rabbi Yitzchak, explains that the Torah

53
00:02:28,031 --> 00:02:31,244
opens with creation to establish that God created

54
00:02:31,284 --> 00:02:34,604
the entire world and therefore has the authority

55
00:02:34,684 --> 00:02:36,924
to give any land to any nation He

56
00:02:37,004 --> 00:02:37,724
chooses.

57
00:02:38,151 --> 00:02:40,764
If the nations of the world ever say

58
00:02:40,817 --> 00:02:44,204
to Israel, "You are thieves, you conquered the

59
00:02:44,284 --> 00:02:48,364
land from other peoples," Israel can reply, "The

60
00:02:48,431 --> 00:02:50,364
whole earth belongs to God.

61
00:02:50,657 --> 00:02:53,404
He created it and gave it to whomever

62
00:02:53,484 --> 00:02:57,484
He saw fit." This is a remarkable opening

63
00:02:57,564 --> 00:02:58,764
move by Rashi.

64
00:02:58,947 --> 00:03:01,724
Before we even get into the details of

65
00:03:01,764 --> 00:03:04,364
the creation narrative, he's asking us to think

66
00:03:04,404 --> 00:03:07,244
about the purpose of the text itself —

67
00:03:07,324 --> 00:03:09,724
why it's structured the way it is, and

68
00:03:09,772 --> 00:03:13,004
what theological claim it's making about God's sovereignty

69
00:03:13,068 --> 00:03:16,729
over the world.Next, let's look at the creation

70
00:03:16,782 --> 00:03:17,929
of humanity.

71
00:03:18,109 --> 00:03:21,770
The Torah says in chapter one, verse twenty-six,

72
00:03:22,010 --> 00:03:25,850
"Let us make man in our image." That

73
00:03:25,930 --> 00:03:29,290
plural — "let us" — is startling.

74
00:03:29,610 --> 00:03:32,490
If God is one, who is He speaking

75
00:03:32,597 --> 00:03:32,890
to?

76
00:03:33,263 --> 00:03:36,970
Rashi acknowledges that this language could be misused

77
00:03:37,050 --> 00:03:39,610
to suggest that multiple divine beings were involved

78
00:03:39,663 --> 00:03:40,650
in creation.

79
00:03:40,850 --> 00:03:43,290
But he explains that God was speaking to

80
00:03:43,310 --> 00:03:46,570
the angels, consulting with them out of humility,

81
00:03:46,597 --> 00:03:49,210
to teach proper conduct — that a greater

82
00:03:49,277 --> 00:03:51,850
being should consult with and seek the agreement

83
00:03:51,903 --> 00:03:54,010
of lesser ones before acting.

84
00:03:54,357 --> 00:03:57,530
Rashi adds that even though this phrasing risks

85
00:03:57,591 --> 00:04:01,307
misunderstanding, the Torah uses it anyway because the

86
00:04:01,357 --> 00:04:04,347
ethical lesson about humility is too important to

87
00:04:04,443 --> 00:04:05,147
omit.

88
00:04:05,327 --> 00:04:07,467
And he points out that the very next

89
00:04:07,534 --> 00:04:10,267
verse switches to the singular — "And God

90
00:04:10,347 --> 00:04:13,707
created man" — making clear that God alone

91
00:04:13,767 --> 00:04:14,987
did the creating.

92
00:04:15,179 --> 00:04:19,227
This is classic Rashi: he spots the difficulty,

93
00:04:19,307 --> 00:04:22,667
acknowledges the risk of misreading, and then shows

94
00:04:22,707 --> 00:04:26,568
how the text itself resolves the tension.Moving to

95
00:04:26,608 --> 00:04:29,288
the Garden of Eden story, after Adam and

96
00:04:29,388 --> 00:04:32,568
Eve eat from the forbidden tree, God pronounces

97
00:04:32,648 --> 00:04:34,808
consequences for each of them.

98
00:04:35,048 --> 00:04:38,088
To the woman, God says in chapter three,

99
00:04:38,128 --> 00:04:41,608
verse sixteen, "And to your husband will be

100
00:04:41,672 --> 00:04:45,128
your desire, and he will rule over you."

101
00:04:45,581 --> 00:04:50,008
Rashi reads this as describing a specific psychological

102
00:04:50,068 --> 00:04:53,928
and social dynamic: the woman will desire her

103
00:04:53,998 --> 00:04:57,048
husband for intimacy, yet she will not have

104
00:04:57,088 --> 00:05:00,728
the boldness to express that desire openly —

105
00:05:00,797 --> 00:05:03,528
rather, the initiative will come from him.

106
00:05:03,795 --> 00:05:06,968
Rashi is doing something subtle here.

107
00:05:07,181 --> 00:05:09,608
He's reading the verse not just as a

108
00:05:09,703 --> 00:05:12,968
punishment but as a description of a relational

109
00:05:13,048 --> 00:05:16,728
imbalance, a shift in the dynamic between man

110
00:05:16,768 --> 00:05:19,528
and woman that results from the transgression in

111
00:05:19,548 --> 00:05:20,408
the garden.

112
00:05:20,701 --> 00:05:23,368
It's a comment that invites us to reflect

113
00:05:23,421 --> 00:05:27,448
on power, vulnerability, and the consequences of human

114
00:05:27,538 --> 00:05:30,872
choices.Now let's turn to one of the most

115
00:05:30,934 --> 00:05:33,512
dramatic moments in the portion — the story

116
00:05:33,592 --> 00:05:35,032
of Cain and Abel.

117
00:05:35,219 --> 00:05:38,979
After God accepts Abel's offering but not Cain's,

118
00:05:39,085 --> 00:05:42,076
the Torah says in chapter four, verse eight,

119
00:05:42,356 --> 00:05:45,596
"And Cain said to Abel his brother," and

120
00:05:45,660 --> 00:05:48,396
then, in the Hebrew text, there's a striking

121
00:05:48,496 --> 00:05:50,876
gap — the verse never tells us what

122
00:05:50,956 --> 00:05:52,316
Cain actually said.

123
00:05:52,503 --> 00:05:55,196
It jumps straight to "and it came to

124
00:05:55,292 --> 00:05:57,356
pass when they were in the field, that

125
00:05:57,436 --> 00:05:59,996
Cain rose up against his brother Abel and

126
00:06:00,065 --> 00:06:03,596
killed him." Rashi explains that Cain picked a

127
00:06:03,660 --> 00:06:06,556
quarrel with Abel, seeking a pretext to fight

128
00:06:06,588 --> 00:06:07,036
with him.

129
00:06:07,140 --> 00:06:10,956
According to one midrashic tradition Rashi cites, they

130
00:06:10,988 --> 00:06:13,116
were arguing about how to divide the world

131
00:06:13,176 --> 00:06:13,836
between them.

132
00:06:14,156 --> 00:06:17,116
Rashi fills in the silence of the text,

133
00:06:17,166 --> 00:06:19,600
showing us that the Torah's gap is itself

134
00:06:19,658 --> 00:06:22,560
meaningful — the content of the argument almost

135
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:23,360
doesn't matter.

136
00:06:23,552 --> 00:06:26,960
What matters is that Cain entered the conversation

137
00:06:27,008 --> 00:06:28,480
with violent intent.

138
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:32,160
The missing words become a literary device, and

139
00:06:32,213 --> 00:06:35,737
Rashi draws our attention to it.Finally, at the

140
00:06:35,801 --> 00:06:38,137
very end of the portion, in chapter six,

141
00:06:38,177 --> 00:06:41,500
verse eight, the Torah tells us, "And Noah

142
00:06:41,553 --> 00:06:43,900
found grace in the eyes of the Lord."

143
00:06:44,012 --> 00:06:47,020
This single verse serves as a bridge between

144
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:49,740
the total corruption of humanity and the story

145
00:06:49,793 --> 00:06:51,740
of the flood that will follow in next

146
00:06:51,804 --> 00:06:52,700
week's portion.

147
00:06:52,940 --> 00:06:56,300
Rashi's comment here is brief but important —

148
00:06:56,327 --> 00:06:57,980
he notes that this is a statement of

149
00:06:58,060 --> 00:07:01,820
divine favor, that amidst an entire generation marked

150
00:07:01,860 --> 00:07:05,820
for destruction, one man's righteousness caught God's attention.

151
00:07:05,980 --> 00:07:08,140
It sets up the tension that will drive

152
00:07:08,180 --> 00:07:10,380
the next portion: what does it mean to

153
00:07:10,407 --> 00:07:12,780
be righteous in a corrupt world, and is

154
00:07:12,844 --> 00:07:15,820
Noah's goodness absolute or only relative to his

155
00:07:15,885 --> 00:07:16,620
generation?

156
00:07:16,807 --> 00:07:19,820
Rashi will explore that question more fully next

157
00:07:19,884 --> 00:07:22,060
week, but even here, at the close of

158
00:07:22,120 --> 00:07:27,122
Bereshit, he plants the seed of that inquiry.Stepping

159
00:07:27,186 --> 00:07:29,682
back to look at Rashi's commentary across this

160
00:07:29,762 --> 00:07:33,762
entire portion, several themes emerge.

161
00:07:33,842 --> 00:07:37,442
First, Rashi is deeply concerned with the purpose

162
00:07:37,502 --> 00:07:40,002
and structure of the text — not just

163
00:07:40,034 --> 00:07:42,322
what it says, but why it says it

164
00:07:42,375 --> 00:07:45,282
in this particular way and in this particular

165
00:07:45,349 --> 00:07:46,322
order.

166
00:07:46,482 --> 00:07:49,523
His very first comment asks why the Torah

167
00:07:49,580 --> 00:07:52,643
begins where it does, setting up a hermeneutical

168
00:07:52,699 --> 00:07:56,163
principle that nothing in the text is accidental.

169
00:07:56,403 --> 00:08:01,523
Second, Rashi pays extraordinary attention to the small

170
00:08:01,633 --> 00:08:06,323
details — a plural pronoun, a missing quotation,

171
00:08:06,376 --> 00:08:09,443
an unusual phrase — and shows how these

172
00:08:09,539 --> 00:08:12,083
tiny features carry enormous weight.

173
00:08:12,363 --> 00:08:17,283
Third, he's unafraid to bring in midrashic traditions

174
00:08:17,390 --> 00:08:20,083
to fill gaps in the narrative, but he

175
00:08:20,131 --> 00:08:23,443
does so carefully, always in service of explaining

176
00:08:23,523 --> 00:08:25,603
a genuine difficulty in the text.

177
00:08:25,903 --> 00:08:30,483
And finally, Rashi reads these ancient stories with

178
00:08:30,563 --> 00:08:33,603
a keen eye for human psychology — the

179
00:08:33,683 --> 00:08:37,043
shame of Eve, the rage of Cain, the

180
00:08:37,123 --> 00:08:40,643
humility modeled by God — reminding us that

181
00:08:40,663 --> 00:08:43,605
the Torah is not just telling history but

182
00:08:43,658 --> 00:08:48,488
teaching us about the human condition.Thank you so

183
00:08:48,552 --> 00:08:51,048
much for joining me for this first episode

184
00:08:51,128 --> 00:08:53,048
on Parashat Bereshit.

185
00:08:53,195 --> 00:08:57,208
Until next week, keep reading, keep questioning, and

186
00:08:57,288 --> 00:09:00,328
keep learning — that's what Rashi would want

187
00:09:00,408 --> 00:09:01,368
us to do.

